  "result": "I've added the `refunded` order state. The test suite passes (11 tests), and nothing is committed.\n\n- **`shop/orders.py`**: `refunded` is now a valid state. Paid and shipped orders can move to it, and nothing can move out of it.\n- **`tests/test_orders.py`**: five new tests:\n  - paid \u2192 refunded is allowed.\n  - shipped \u2192 refunded is allowed.\n  - new \u2192 refunded and cancelled \u2192 refunded are rejected.\n  - refunded \u2192 anything (including refunded) is rejected.\n  - `sales_by_customer` leaves out refunded orders. The test uses one customer with paid, shipped and refunded orders, and another whose only order is refunded.\n\n**`sales_by_customer` itself didn't need changing.** In `shop/reports.py` it already counts only paid and shipped orders, so refunded ones are left out automatically. The new test locks that in. I searched the rest of the repo for other lists of states that would need the new one, such as storage, the README, or a database schema, and found none.\n\nTo run the tests: `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .`. The run left untracked `__pycache__` folders under `shop/` and `tests/`.",
